===Phrase===
{{head|en|phrase}}

# {{lb|en|UK|humorous}} Used to [[highlight]] a [[sexual]] [[ambiguity]] in an [[innocent]] [[remark]].
#: {{ux|en|Heavens, that's a big one – '''as the actress said to the bishop'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1976|title=The Left in Britain|author=David Widgery
|passage=The important thing was where you do it, not what you do ('''as the actress said to the bishop''')}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|title=The Entertainers: portraits of stardom in the twentieth century|author=Timothy White
|passage="Oh, well," offered Andrews wryly, as she peeled a piece of fruit, "nothing like a hot banana — '''as the actress said to the bishop'''." She then apologized for the tacky sexual quip, explaining, "It just slipped out — '''as the actress said to the bishop'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|title=Ghostly demarcations: a symposium on Jacques Derrida's Spectres of Marx|author=Michael Sprinker
|passage=He has, '''as the actress said to the bishop''', been an unconscionably long time coming,{{...}}}}

